Why this feels fundamental

Looking back, many of your Pantheon ideas already reduce to this:

* Semantic Zoom
* Adaptive Resolution
* LiDAR
* Rangefinder
* Scout Navigation
* Mirror
* Hammer
* Translation

They all depend on selecting the appropriate resolution.

Resolution Control might actually sit above them as the meta-operator that decides which operator to invoke and at what granularity.

⸻

I’d even refine the name slightly.

Resolution Control is good because it’s intuitive.

But architecturally, I think the deeper primitive is:

Resolution Modulation

Control sounds static.

Modulation implies continuous adjustment in response to feedback—more like autofocus, adaptive bitrate streaming, or the human visual system shifting between peripheral vision and foveal focus.

That seems to capture what you’ve been describing throughout the Pantheon: not finding the “right” level once, but continuously tuning resolution to match the problem while preserving the invariants that matter.